VeridianOS: KCharSelectData: check QFile::open, read the data pointer once

GCC reported, for the VeridianOS cross build:

- src/kcharselectdata.cpp:89: ignoring return value of QFile::open
  [-Wunused-result]. When the embedded resource cannot be opened, readAll()
  returns an empty array and the existing "size < 40" check rejects it, so
  the outcome was already "return false"; the failure is now handled where it
  happens. No behaviour change.
- QtCore/qendian.h:57: array subscript 4..7 is outside array bounds of
  'const char [1]' [-Warray-bounds] (4x), reached through qFromLittleEndian()
  inlined into KCharSelectData::sectionList(), blockName() and sectionName().
  These three functions called dataFile.constData() twice, once for the
  header reads and once for the string table. constData() returns
  &QByteArray::_empty (a 1-byte object) when the array has no buffer; GCC
  jump-threaded the two calls into a path where the header reads at offset
  16..28 go through &_empty, and warned about that path. It is not reachable
  (openDataFile() only succeeds with at least 40 bytes loaded), but the code
  now takes the pointer once and derives both views from it, which removes
  the duplicated path. The warning was located in Qt's installed header but
  caused by this code; nothing in Qt needs changing. No behaviour change.

